# agent-session-forecast

**How long will this AI-agent session run, and how much should we trust that guess?**

When someone starts a coding-agent session (Claude Code, Cline, Kilo, OpenCode, Hermes...), it might end after
two requests or two hundred. If you run an inference service: it decides how you schedule GPUs,
cache prompts and plan capacity. This project asks how well we can predict a session's total length from only
its **first few requests**, and, more importantly, whether the usual way of measuring that is lying to us.

It works on the public [FreeInference Agentic Trace](https://huggingface.co/datasets/harvardMadsys/freeinference_agentic_trace)
(`harvardMadsys/freeinference_agentic_trace` on Hugging Face). No data is stored in this repo.

---

## The short version of the idea

Most forecasting write-ups split their sessions randomly into train and test. That's fine if sessions are
independent. They aren't: a handful of accounts produce most of the sessions, and each account has its *own habits*
(some people always run long sessions, some always run short ones). A random split lets the model recognise
the account in the test set, so it looks better than it will on someone it has never met.

So this repo evaluates the same models three ways and compares them honestly:

| Design | What it simulates | What it answers |
|---|---|---|
| **Random** | shuffle sessions | "how good is it on accounts we already know?" |
| **Account-grouped** | hold out whole accounts | "how good is it on a **brand-new** user?" |
| **Temporal** | train on early week, test on late week | "how good is it **tomorrow**?" |

Then it asks the follow-up questions a careful person would ask:

- Is the fancy model (gradient boosting) actually better than just "the median for this tool"?
- Is the gap between random and grouped real, or just noise from having few accounts?
- Do the prediction intervals really cover 90% of the time on new accounts, for every tool, for every account?
- Do they still work as the week drifts along?

---

## What you get

Run it once and you get two dashboards, a plain-text summary and a handful of CSVs. Since v0.2 the output folder is organised as
`figures/` (PNG + PDF per figure), `tables/` (CSV + LaTeX), `extras/<analysis>/`, `run_info/` (provenance) and, with extras on, `dashboard3.png`.

**Dashboard 1: the profile, the forecast error, the intervals**

![dashboard 1](examples/synthetic_run/dashboard.png)

**Dashboard 2: is the signal real?** (paired tests, which features matter, drift)

![dashboard 2](examples/synthetic_run/dashboard2.png)

**Dashboard 3: the optional extra analyses** (`--all-extras`)

![dashboard 3](examples/synthetic_run/dashboard3.png)

Every figure is also saved on its own (PNG + PDF) in `figures/`; samples are in
[`examples/synthetic_run/figures/`](examples/synthetic_run/figures/).

> These pictures come from the built-in **synthetic** data, which exists only to test the code.
> The shapes are illustrative; the numbers say nothing about the real dataset.
> See [`examples/synthetic_run/summary.txt`](examples/synthetic_run/summary.txt) for the matching text output.

